            <ArticleTitle>Improving the induction characteristics of methohexitone. A study of the effect of adding thiopentone to methohexitone.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>338-40</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A double-blind, randomised, controlled study was undertaken to investigate the effects on injection pain and excitatory phenomena of adding thiopentone to methohexitone. One hundred and twenty unpremedicated, day-case patients received one of four induction regimens: unmodified 1% methohexitone, or a mixture of 2.5% thiopentone and 1% methohexitone in ratios of 1:1, 1:3 or 1:9 by volume. The addition of one part of thiopentone to three parts of methohexitone produced a mixture which significantly reduced the occurrence of pain and discomfort compared with methohexitone alone from 67% to 13% (p &lt; 0.01) and reduced the occurrence of excitatory effects from 50% to 13% (p &lt; 0.01).</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
